# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""The translation tables for every language after French.

One entry per English key - the key is the English text exactly as it is
written in the code - and the seven translations side by side, in the order

    de   es   it   nl   pt   ru   ja

so a word can be checked against its neighbours as it is written, and a new
string is added to all seven in one line.

One hard rule: **a translation must keep its format specifiers, in the same
order**. The line goes to format(), which is plain printf - it reads its
arguments in the order the specifiers appear, so swapping "%d of %zu" round
hands an int to a %zu, and adding a second %s reads an argument nobody passed.
Japanese would rather put the total before the count and the month before the
day; doing that needs positional specifiers (%2$zu), which this build has not
been tested with, so the English order is kept and the wording works around
it. tools/i18n_scan.py --check refuses a mismatch. An empty string means "not done
yet": tools/gen_lang_tables.py leaves that language's row out of the file and
tr() falls back to the English.

The file that gets *shipped* is source/core/lang_<code>.cpp, generated from
here. Run tools/gen_lang_tables.py after editing, and
tools/i18n_scan.py --all to see where each language has got to.
"""

ORDER = ("de", "es", "it", "nl", "pt", "ru", "ja")

